        BRIdiomItem(idiom: 'stealing their wonder', definition: 'Removing the natural sense of curiosity, magic, and open possibility that belongs to childhood.', examples: [
        'Constant testing and ranking is quietly stealing their wonder about the world.',
        'When every activity is measured, we end up stealing their wonder.',
        'A healthy childhood protects rather than steals their wonder.',
      ]),
      ],
      arguments: [
        BRArgumentItem(claim: 'Excessive academic pressure from an early age is producing record levels of anxiety and depression in children.', explanation: 'Multiple large-scale studies from the United Kingdom, the United States, South Korea, and China show sharp rises in clinical anxiety, self-harm, and depressive symptoms among school-age children. The common factor is not poverty or family breakdown alone, but the intensity of academic competition that begins in primary years. Children as young as seven report fear of failure, sleep loss, and feelings of worthlessness linked to test scores. In South Korea, the extreme pressure of the education system has been linked to some of the highest youth suicide rates among developed nations. When childhood becomes a continuous performance review, the natural joy of learning is replaced by fear. Governments and parents who continue to intensify early competition are not preparing stronger adults; they are manufacturing a generation of psychologically damaged young people. Real childhood requires space for failure, recovery, and unmeasured growth.'),
        BRArgumentItem(claim: 'Digital technology is replacing real play, real friendship, and real attention, with measurable harm to development.', explanation: 'Research from the past decade demonstrates that heavy screen use in early and middle childhood is associated with shorter attention spans, weaker social skills, delayed language development, and higher rates of anxiety. Children who once spent hours outdoors inventing games now spend those hours scrolling, gaming, or watching short videos. The American Academy of Pediatrics and similar bodies have issued clear warnings about the displacement of unstructured play. Friendship has also changed: many children report having online contacts but few deep, face-to-face relationships. The result is a generation that is more connected digitally yet more lonely and less resilient offline. Protecting childhood means deliberately limiting the invasion of screens so that the brain and the personality can develop through real-world experience.'),
        BRArgumentItem(claim: 'Free, unstructured play is not optional entertainment; it is a biological and psychological necessity.', explanation: 'Decades of research in developmental psychology, from Piaget to modern neuroscientists, show that free play is how children develop creativity, emotional regulation, problem-solving, and social intelligence. When every hour is scheduled with lessons, tutoring, or organised sport, the brain is denied the very activity it needs for healthy wiring. Countries that still protect long periods of free play, such as Finland in the early school years, consistently produce children with strong academic and emotional outcomes later. In contrast, systems that minimise play in the name of early achievement often produce higher short-term scores and higher long-term mental health costs. Denying children free play is not efficiency; it is developmental neglect dressed up as ambition.'),
        BRArgumentItem(claim: 'The commercialisation of childhood turns children into consumers and performers rather than developing human beings.', explanation: 'From targeted advertising on digital platforms to the early branding of academic success, modern childhood is saturated with commercial messages. Children are encouraged to measure their worth by possessions, appearance, grades, and online popularity. This creates a constant sense of inadequacy and comparison. Studies of advertising exposure show that it increases materialistic values and decreases life satisfaction even in young children. When the market treats childhood as a sales opportunity, the protective space that children need disappears. A society that allows this commercial invasion cannot simultaneously claim to value the well-being of its youngest members.'),
        BRArgumentItem(claim: 'Early specialisation and intense competition produce burnout rather than excellence.', explanation: 'In sport, music, and academics, the trend toward earlier and more intense specialisation has been linked to higher rates of injury, anxiety, and eventual dropout. Research on elite youth athletes and high-achieving students shows that many of the most pressured children leave their field by late adolescence, exhausted and disillusioned. In contrast, children who experience a broad, playful foundation often develop deeper and more sustainable motivation later. The belief that starting earlier and pushing harder always produces better results is not supported by long-term evidence. It produces more casualties than champions. Protecting a broad and unpressured childhood is therefore not anti-excellence; it is the more intelligent route to lasting high performance.'),
        BRArgumentItem(claim: 'Children need adult protection from adult problems, not early exposure to them.', explanation: 'Social media and 24-hour news cycles expose children to climate anxiety, political conflict, violence, and sexual content long before they have the emotional tools to process them. Developmental psychologists emphasise that children need a protected period in which the world feels fundamentally safe and manageable. When that protection is removed, the result is not informed young citizens but frightened and overwhelmed children. Real-life examples include the measurable rise in climate anxiety among primary-school pupils and the documented impact of online pornography on early sexual attitudes. A responsible society filters the adult world for children rather than streaming it directly into their developing minds.'),
        BRArgumentItem(claim: 'The loss of boredom and solitude is damaging creativity and inner life.', explanation: 'Boredom has historically been the space in which children invent games, tell themselves stories, and develop an inner world. Continuous digital stimulation removes that space. Neuroscience research indicates that the brain\'s default mode network, important for imagination and self-reflection, is less active when attention is constantly captured by external devices. Teachers and parents increasingly report children who cannot generate their own ideas or entertain themselves without a screen. By filling every moment, modern life is impoverishing the very capacity for original thought that education claims to value. Restoring periods of unstructured time is therefore an educational necessity, not a luxury.'),
        BRArgumentItem(claim: 'Rising rates of sleep deprivation among children are a direct result of over-scheduling and screen use.', explanation: 'Sleep is essential for memory consolidation, emotional regulation, and physical growth. Yet surveys in multiple countries show that a large proportion of school-age children are chronically short of sleep. The causes are clear: early school start times, heavy homework loads, late-evening tutoring, and screen use that delays melatonin production. The consequences include poorer academic performance, higher obesity rates, and increased emotional instability. No amount of extra tuition can compensate for a brain that is not allowed to rest. Protecting childhood requires protecting sleep, which means reducing both academic load and evening screen time.'),
        BRArgumentItem(claim: 'The current model treats childhood as preparation for economic productivity rather than as a valuable stage of life in itself.', explanation: 'Much of modern education and parenting language frames childhood as a race to produce employable adults. Play is justified only if it builds skills, rest is allowed only if it improves performance, and joy is secondary to measurable outcomes. This instrumental view empties childhood of intrinsic worth. Philosophical and psychological traditions from Rousseau to contemporary child development research insist that childhood has value in its own right. A society that cannot see children as more than future workers has already lost its moral centre. Defending childhood means defending the right of children to exist as children, not merely as apprentices to adulthood.'),
        BRArgumentItem(claim: 'Countries and cultures that still protect longer, freer childhoods produce healthier and often more capable young adults.', explanation: 'Comparative evidence from education systems that delay formal academic pressure, such as those in parts of Scandinavia, shows strong later outcomes in both well-being and achievement. Children who experience more play-based early years often display better social skills, greater intrinsic motivation, and comparable or superior academic results by secondary school. In contrast, systems famous for extreme early pressure frequently report high youth mental health costs alongside their academic rankings. The data suggest that protecting childhood is not a barrier to national success; it is one of its more reliable foundations. Societies that remember this produce not only better test scores but better human beings.'),
      ],
    ),
